Revision history for App-Actions - run GitHub Actions workflows locally

0.01    2026-10-19
    - Initial release.
    - Load GitHub Actions workflow files with YAML::PP.
    - Expand build matrices, including include and exclude rules.
    - Run Linux jobs in Docker containers with service containers.
    - Support workflow, job, and step environment variables, GITHUB_ENV,
      GITHUB_PATH, GITHUB_OUTPUT, and step summaries.
    - Support common expression functions and if conditions.
    - Map actions/checkout and shogo82148/actions-setup-perl to local
      equivalents, and run docker:// actions.
    - Remove containers, networks, and pulled images at the end of a run.
    - Run local composite actions, including nested ones, with inputs,
      outputs, github.action_path, and GITHUB_ACTION_PATH.
    - Run local JavaScript actions with node, INPUT_* variables, and pre and
      post steps.
    - Run a Docker wrapper script written in Perl with the current Perl
      interpreter, so the test suite works on Windows.
    - Capture command output without fork() or a piped open(), which Windows
      does not reliably support, and use the same code for git.
    - Make the test suite Windows-safe: no POSIX-only paths, permissions or
      shell commands, and CRLF-tolerant output matching.
    - Write step scripts, the sudo and apt shims, and event.json with LF line
      endings even on Windows, where bash rejects CRLF scripts.  The fake
      shell used by the tests reads step scripts byte for byte and fails on a
      CR, so a CRLF regression is caught there rather than only on Linux.
    - Quote command arguments for the Windows command line, because Perl
      leaves an argument containing a double quote unquoted and the C runtime
      then splits it at its spaces.  This is why the tests no longer pass
      program code through "perl -e".
    - Add an opt-in test and a workflow_dispatch CI job that run one workflow
      against a real Linux Docker daemon, including from a Windows host.
    - Run the environment probes automatically before a workflow, using the
      image the first job will use.  They are silent when they pass and stop
      the run with a report when they do not; --skip-check starts the workflow
      anyway.
    - Prepare every image the run needs - job images, service images and
      docker:// action images, with matrix values resolved - before the first
      container starts, so a mistyped image is reported instead of failing
      halfway through a job.  Local composite actions, including nested ones,
      are read during the same step, so images named inside them are prepared
      too rather than discovered when the action first runs.  The missing
      images are pulled a few at a time rather than one after another, so a
      wide matrix does not serialise its downloads; a failure is still
      reported against the image that needs it.
    - Add "action-run preflight <workflow.yml>", which makes the pre-run
      checks and prepares the images without starting a job, and its
      "--json" form: the same document as "check --json" with an "images"
      array naming each image, the job, service or action that needs it, and
      whether it was pulled, already present, failed or skipped.  Also add
      App::Actions->preflight, App::Actions::Check->summary, and
      App::Actions::Process::run_parallel output redirection so that a report
      can be built without a pull's progress mixed into it.
    - Add t/real-docker-preflight.t, an opt-in test that runs the preflight
      against a real daemon, written for a Windows host with Docker Desktop:
      a probe that cannot run its container, the job, service and docker://
      action images being pulled before the first container, and an
      unpullable image reported against the service that needs it.  The
      Windows real-docker workflow runs it before the workflow test.
    - Version the "check --json" and "preflight --json" documents: each
      carries schema_version, from App::Actions::Check->json_schema_version
      and App::Actions::Runner->preflight_schema_version, so an assertion can
      name the shape it was written for.  Check in a schema and an example
      for each in t/fixtures/, and add t/schema.t, which validates the
      examples and the documents the commands print against them and shows
      that an extra key, a missing key, a wrong type, an unknown enum value
      or another version is rejected.
    - Make the image pull concurrency configurable with --pull-concurrency,
      ACTION_RUN_PULL_CONCURRENCY, or App::Actions->new's pull_concurrency
      option, four by default.  The option wins over the environment; a value
      that is not a positive integer is an error rather than a silent fall
      back to four.  Windows still pulls one image at a time.
    - Check each job's images again with the job's own values, before its
      network or containers exist, so an image that comes from another job's
      result is reported before the job starts rather than halfway through
      it.  The preflight reports such an image as deferred instead of
      guessing at a name, and one that only a step can decide is prepared by
      that step before its container.  A job image built from github.* or
      env.* now resolves to the name the job uses, rather than being passed
      to Docker as an expression with the value torn out.
    - Add "action-run check", which reports the host platform, Docker
      connectivity and whether bind mounts of the temporary and project
      directories work, and "action-run check --json", which reports the same
      as one machine-readable object with stable keys for CI to assert on.  Each problem is followed by a remedy naming the tools
      of the host platform, such as starting Docker Desktop, switching it to
      Linux containers, or sharing the drive that holds the directory.
    - Add "action-run-smoke", the documented end-to-end check: it runs the
      check above and then one workflow against the real daemon, and it is
      what to run on Windows against Docker Desktop.  The Windows CI job runs
      it instead of its own preflight.
    - Run the whole test suite a second time from a temporary directory whose
      path contains spaces, on every platform, so that paths with spaces are
      not left to chance.  SPACE_PATH=0 skips that run.
    - Pull each image once per run with --pull: the images prepared before the
      first job are remembered, so the check a job makes of its own image, a
      second matrix job using the same image, and a step naming an image
      another step already used do not download it again.
    - Add "action-run schema <check|preflight>", which prints the JSON schema
      of the corresponding document, so a CI job can fetch the contract
      instead of copying it.  The schemas now live in App::Actions::Check and
      App::Actions::Runner, which serve them; the duplicate copies in
      t/fixtures/ are gone, the examples remain, and t/schema.t validates the
      documents against the schemas the command prints.
    - Report a service or job container that stops with the status it exited
      with and the last lines it printed, both streams, instead of only saying
      that it did not stay running.  The job container used to stream its logs
      and give a bare failure, so a job that could not start said nothing about
      whether the image, the entrypoint or the command was at fault; it now
      reads the status and the output before removing the container, as the
      services do.  App::Actions::Process::capture can merge the two streams,
      and App::Actions::Docker gained container_exit_code and
      container_log_text for it; the fake Docker can now model a container
      that exits, with its output.
    - Fail the run when a service container restarts or backs off instead of
      ignoring its restart policy.  Docker reports such a container as running
      between attempts, so asking only whether it was running let the steps
      talk to one that kept dying; the restart count and state are read when
      the service starts and again after the job's steps, and the failure
      reports the count with the last lines the container printed.
      App::Actions::Docker gained container_status and container_restart_count,
      and the fake Docker can report a restarting container.
    - Add "action-run --explain", an opt-in dry run whose output is the
      containers rather than the Docker commands.  It starts nothing and needs
      no daemon, and prints one block per job, service and docker:// action
      container saying what each would be told to run, including the
      entrypoint a service's options replace, so a service that stops on
      start-up can be diagnosed without running it.  The runner gained an
      explain option and the Docker wrapper stops printing its commands for it.
    - Hold the checked-in examples to every key in each shipped schema:
      t/schema.t walks both schemas against t/fixtures/check.json,
      check-unready.json, preflight.json and preflight-unready.json and fails
      when a property, a required key or a oneOf alternative appears in none
      of them, so a key the code stopped printing or the schema kept is
      caught rather than left unreachable.  The unready examples are new and
      carry the issues, failed images and mount mismatch a failing report
      produces.
    - Add "action-run-smoke --scenario deferred", an opt-in smoke scenario
      whose workflow has an image only a step can name, so the deferred report
      is exercised end to end against a real daemon rather than only against
      the fake one.  The scenario fails if the preflight did not report the
      image as deferred, and the fake Docker now understands the
      "echo name=value >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT" idiom the scenario is written in.
    - Name the schema version in the check and preflight text reports, on a
      "schema" line before the verdict, so a pasted report says which contract
      it was written against.  The preflight names its own document version and
      leaves the check version out of the report it embeds, and the smoke test,
      which prints no document, leaves it out too.
    - Add "action-run validate <check|preflight> <document.json>", which checks
      a saved document against the schema the distribution ships, without
      Docker or a workflow, so a CI job can validate a result from another job
      or machine.  A mismatch, including a file that is not JSON, exits 70;
      an unknown name or an unreadable file stays a usage error.
    - Hold check --json and preflight --json to those schemas before printing
      them.  The validator now lives in App::Actions::Schema, which both
      commands and t/schema.t share, and a document that no longer matches
      its schema is reported on standard error and exits 70, EX_SOFTWARE,
      instead of being handed to a CI job.  The status is deliberately not 2,
      so a CI job can tell a broken document from a host it has to fix; the
      exception is an App::Actions::Schema::Invalid object, which t/schema.t
      checks from a copy of the tree with a drifted schema.  The first document to trip it was a preflight with
      --skip-check, which reported an empty host; the host needs no probe, so
      it is now reported there too.
    - Report the pull concurrency the preflight used in its JSON document, so
      a CI job can assert that --pull-concurrency or
      ACTION_RUN_PULL_CONCURRENCY reached the puller rather than inferring it
      from the timing of the pulls.  The number is the one handed to the
      puller, which is 1 on Windows whatever was configured, and the preflight
      document is version 2 as a result.
